commit 48893639f8c03240da7e0fd58c3b4fdee01367b9
Author: NOGUCHI Shoji <github.com@jpng.jp>
Date:   Mon Sep 28 17:36:57 2026 +0900

    Issue an abort's continuation before its expert reads are in
    
    An abort used to repair first - read the missing experts, install them -
    and only then encode and commit the continuation, so the GPU sat idle for
    the reads and again for the 1.34 ms a token between the repair returning
    and the continuation starting. With DS4_METAL_V41_GATE_ENCODE_AHEAD=2 the
    repair runs on a thread of its own and the continuation is committed at
    once: the shared expert, the routed gate/up of the resident lanes and the
    down of the slots before the first missing one run while the reads are in
    flight; the buffer then waits on a shared event, which the host signals
    once the reads are installed, and the missing lanes' gate/up and the rest
    of the down follow. Layers with no miss are not touched.
    
    The down is split by lane, not by slot output: a new kernel, the masked
    sum6 body copied rather than shared so the shipping kernel is not
    recompiled, stores each lane's running sums and the part after the wait
    loads them, so every lane adds the same values in the same order and ends
    with the same simd_sum. =3 checks every split down against the one-part
    kernel: 10506 of 10506 identical over 2048 tokens.
    
    Fail-closed: the dispatches behind the wait take their grids from a buffer
    that is zero until the host copies the real grids in, which it does only
    when the repair succeeded; a failed repair still signals, so the buffer
    finishes, and the work behind the wait runs empty. =4 injects a failure:
    that request returns an error and the six after it match main. Only the
    encoding thread's dispatches are gated - the queue keepalive once took a
    slot when this was a global.
    
    Counted, all zero over 2048-token and seven-prompt runs: a selected expert
    evicted while its continuation was in flight, an address zero at release,
    an address, slab slot or slot generation that moved before the buffer
    completed, a grid reused in flight, a continuation on an all-resident
    layer, and a continuation whose routed dispatches differed from what its
    missing mask says (gate/up 1 + resident?1, down 1 + first-miss>0?1).
    
    =5 alternates the two schedules token by token in one process, A-B-B-A
    and B-A-A-B blocks, each token keeping its own misses. Two cooled
    2048-token runs, all identical to main, the arms' work alike (5196 against
    5310 aborts, 57020 against 57124 MiB repaired): ready minus repair-first
    -2.70 and -2.18 ms a token (64-token-window se 0.70 and 0.47), negative in
    both block orders each time; 45.11 -> 42.41 and 44.31 -> 42.13 ms a token.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

